f these russian companies not to the  extent that they're not importing uh the  resources but also that they've said  that they'll reduce russian gas by  two-thirds by the end of the year is  that not enough  how many ukrainians would have died by  the end of the year i think that's the  answer to the question listen because of  the big mistakes we all made in not  reducing energy dependence after the  caesar crimea in 2014 they're in a bind  make no mistake  30 percent of  germany's total primary energy supply  comes from russian oil  gas and coal it's enormous if they make  the stop  the estimated hit is between  one and three percent of gdp or one  thousand euros ahead so it's a big  economic cost  but as i say  are you really going to go on paying  putin with every heating bill  when ukrainians are being massacred in  the way they are but what is the  realistic middle point because you can't  just wean off overnight so  is it realistic for germans or any  europeans to do that  so interestingly the big profit maker  for russia is oil  so one way to do this  i is actually to start by stopping oil  imports and take a bit more time over  gas  um the good news is we're coming into  the summer you don't need so much energy  in the summer but you know what the  popular outrage in germany is so immense  as it is in britain that i think this  may yet happen  and timothy on the military front uh  nato has ruled out this no-fly zone as  zlensky is asking for citing concerns  that putin would use as justification to  attack nato but somehow people have  argued that putin clearly needs no  justification for aggression so it  doesn't really matter almost and if  zalensky's asking for it maybe we should  have that what do you make of that  argument well  what i say is  nato is not going to do it  that's what's clear and i mean they've  been already joe biden has been  absolutely clear about it jens  stoltenberg has been absolutely clear  about it so we could have a hypothetical  discussion about what would happen if  and the answer is  you know putin is capable of anything  but realistically it's not going to  happen and so we have to do everything  short of that mark and actually what i  gather from the experts is that if we  can get in  sophisticated uh uh  anti-aircraft air defense missiles  that's in a way as important as um  getting in those you know polish mig  planes or  a no-fly zone which would be a no-fly  zone only against the russians  and i want to talk to you about this  zielinski plan you're you're writing  about tell us about that  yeah um well i'm fed up with people  keeping saying you know we have a new  marshall plan because you know they've  been about i i can personally remember  about seven proposed martial plans that  never happened for former yugoslavia you  name it for east central europe uh so i  call it the zelinski plan but  listen the the the war damage is already  estimated at well over a hundred billion  dollars that's clearly an underestimated  the russians are still pounding away so  it's hundreds of billions and the least  we in the west can do  is to have a big reconstruction plan  and my argument is by the way that's  also rebuilding the homes to which the  millions of refugees  in the rest of europe will go home so  that's important for us too  but my argument is to connect it to some  intermediate steps of connecting ukraine  more closely to europe well it's a  fascinating idea and there's the  sweetener i guess to those concessions  that you allude to can we talk about how  this will end because it does seem like  zolensky may have to make some of these  painful concessions  so  as i say in the piece  i pray daily for a miracle on that  nipper  like the miracle on the vestila in  1920 when the poles defeated the  advancing red army and and the red army  simply retreated  but that would be a miracle  and barring miracles neither side can  win and therefore they're going to have  to fight their way  i'm afraid through rivers of blood sweat  and tears  to a point where there's what um  lawrence friedman the strategic thinker  calls a hurting stalemate when both  sides are hurting so much that they have  to make a deal and  only the ukrainians have the right to  say what that deal is  you know can be  but we can at least start thinking about  it now zielinski  yesterday  tuesday um we're speaking on wednesday  said  i don't think we're going to become a  member of nato so he's already in effect  saying okay we can go to neutrality  what's really painful is going to be the  territorial concessions crimea and  donetsk and luhans the provinces in the  in the far east of the country  and that that that's really tough to  take  would i mean let alone do you think the  ukrainian government the ukrainian  people would accept something like that  so i talk regularly to people connected  with the ukrainian government at the  moment the public position is no  but and of course  we should in no way shape or form be  kind of forcing this on them that we  have absolutely no right to do that  but privately they will acknowledge  you know these are facts on the ground  the comparison i draw with the so-called  winter war between finland and the  soviet union in 1939-1940  um  stalin thought he'd roll over finland as  putin thought he'd roll over ukraine in  fact the poo the finns put up the most  magnificent amazing military resistance  gave stalin the bloody nose  and basically six months later they  signed a piece  they had to give up about one tenth of  finnish territory brutal concession but  finland remained a free  sovereign independent democratic country  and i think every finn  you speak to would say that was a price  worth paying  and finally timothy i just want to end  on this note that you make about  zelinski um obviously his performance i  think throughout all of this i think  many people can agree has been exceeded  expectations of an actor or comedian  you also mentioned his twitter feed as a  master class in 21st century war  leadership tell us about that  so i  every viewer should  should follow him on twitter it's  absolutely amazing by the way when i  started he was just over 2 million  followers now he's well over 5 million  in the middle of this war in risk of his  life after every conversation with a  western leader biden or schultz or  macron boris johnson or whoever it is he  just has a tweet  saying what they talked about and doing  his main points then he does these video  clips  walking around besieged kiev  his messages to the ukrainian people and  the russian people it is an absolute  master class in political communication  and the effect is you know  the ukrainians have already won the  information war  the russians are still fighting the  military war but the information war  they've just won  hands down so it's like you know imagine  winston churchill with a twitter account  that's a great note to end on timothy  gardenash thank you very much for  joining spectator tv  [Music]  you

